        "description": "<figure>\n\n<img alt=\"Drew Michael on stage in the off-Broadway show Love Me.\" data-caption=\"Love Me uses Auracast broadcasts both for accessibility and the sound design. | Photo: Curtis Brown\" data-portal-copyright=\"Photo: Curtis Brown\" data-has-syndication-rights=\"1\" src=\"https://platform.theverge.com/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/2026/11/love-me-off-broadway-auracast-1.jpg?quality=90&#038;strip=all&#038;crop=0,0,100,100\" />\n\t<figcaption>\n\tLove Me uses Auracast broadcasts both for accessibility and the sound design. | Photo: Curtis Brown\t</figcaption>\n</figure>\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Up a few concrete steps and through the front door of a 200-year-old row house in New York City, I follow a crowd of people into the 178-seat SoHo Playhouse. As we filter to our seats, ushers hand each person a black drawstring bag with a pair of wired headphones connected to <a href=\"https://www.auriaudio.com/products/auri-rx1-receiver/\">a small Auracast receiver</a>. Then, soon after the play begins",
